"id": "written-style",
"el": "Η γραμματική του γραπτού λόγου",
"title": "The grammar of written Greek",
"span": "B2–C1",
"blurb": "What changes when Greek is written formally: the internal augment (υπέγραψα, συνέβη, επέστρεψε), learned passives in -ούμαι and -ώμαι (θεωρείται, εξαρτάται), news forms like εξελέγη and ελήφθη, fixed learned phrases (εν λόγω, εκ των υστέρων), turning verbs into nouns (η αύξηση των τιμών), impersonal passives (θεωρείται ότι, αναμένεται να), and switching register (διότι, προκειμένου να, λαμβάνω).",
"intro": [
{
"p": "Newspapers, official letters, university texts and the B2 reading and writing papers use a layer of Greek that you rarely hear at a kafeneio. It is the same language with **learned** (λόγια) forms inherited from the old written standard, more **nouns** where speech uses verbs, and more **impersonal** constructions. You need to recognise all of it and to produce some of it in a formal letter or essay."
},
